netifd: keep DSA conduit lifecycle with the kernel

A DSA user port reports its conduit as iflink, but the kernel DSA core
owns that conduit: dsa_user_open() opens it and a conduit
NETDEV_GOING_DOWN closes every user port using it. If netifd holds the
conduit as a parent, stopping the last user port brings the conduit
down and closes the other users sharing it.

On kernels >= 6.1 identify DSA user ports with the existing
system_if_get_master_ifindex() helper and return no parent. Ordinary
VLAN/other netdev parent discovery and the pre-6.1 path are unchanged.

--- a/system-linux.c
+++ b/system-linux.c
@@ -3478,9 +3478,22 @@
 {
 	char buf[64], *devname;
 	int ifindex, iflink;
 
+#if LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(6,1,0)
+	/*
+	 * A DSA user port reports its conduit as iflink, but the kernel DSA
+	 * core owns that conduit: dsa_user_open() opens it and a conduit
+	 * NETDEV_GOING_DOWN closes every user port using it. If netifd holds
+	 * the conduit as a parent, stopping the last user port brings the
+	 * conduit down and closes the other users sharing it, so leave the
+	 * conduit lifecycle to the kernel.
+	 */
+	if (system_if_get_master_ifindex(dev) > 0)
+		return NULL;
+#endif
+
 	if (system_get_dev_sysfs("iflink", dev->ifname, buf, sizeof(buf)) < 0)
 		return NULL;
 
 	iflink = strtoul(buf, NULL, 0);
 	ifindex = system_if_resolve(dev);
